\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Dollarization remains a risk for Uruguay\b0\par\par\uc2 1,489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 LUIS CUSTODIO Uruguayan economist Javier Bianchi - Senior Researcher at the Federal Reserve of Minnneapolis (*) - along with a student at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , Jorge Mondrag\u243\'00\'F3n, recently developed research that shows that the inability to use monetary policy for macroeconomic stabilization leaves to a government more vulnerable to a liquidity crisis. "A government with monetary autonomy can stabilize the economy and can easily remain immune to a maturity crisis," explains Bianchi. He understands that these risks are also present in dollarized economies such as Uruguay. He believes that greater management of macroprudential regulation puts countries like ours less exposed to this type of problem. Coming up a summary of the interview.\par \par \uc2  - Did the lack of monetary autonomy play a key role in the last major crisis in Europe?>> - We highlighted the importance of monetary policy to determine how exposed a government is to a roll over crisis; Many of the works carried out in this area were previously motivated in Mexico, which had a debt of 270 days, but today European countries have a maturity in their debt of 5 to 7 years, so the result is that if these economies If they were in a position to use their monetary policy, they would be quite immune to a roll-over crisis, but because they are tied to \uc2 EU\uc2  policies, they are very exposed. When there is a recession, you can not count on this monetary policy tool. In this context, investors anticipate that if the government pays the debt that will have a recessive effect so the government will be more costly to pay the debt. Therefore, they are more prone to "run" when they perceive that other investors are not willing to lend to the government. They generate something like a bank run, but for governments.>> - You tested your theory with data from Spain from 1996 to 2015 ...>> - The case of Spain is interesting because it has had very high levels of unemployment and a very strong recession, and the spreads at the time were at levels of 500 basis points. If one compares it with other countries like England, despite having higher debt levels, it had clearly lower SpreadS. According to our study, that would be a product of the independence of the monetary policy of England, unlike Spain. - What lessons does this process leave?>> - What suggests for the framework of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  is that there should be an institutional policy of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to somehow act as a lender of last resort in such situations. In a way he did it, spontaneously and informally, when \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  said he would do whatever it took to preserve the Euro. The ECB president said it and had an impact on the market, but at the time there was a lot of resistance, particularly from Germany, so it was not clear to what extent the \uc2 ECB\uc2  was going to rescue these countries, which generated a lot of uncertainty. That is why we understand that this should be more institutionalized and not be left to political push. >> - What result would arise from applying in Uruguay the logic used in that study?>> - In Uruguay we have a flexible exchange rate. Even so, there are limitations to the activity of monetary policy, particularly because the Uruguayan economy is highly dollarized. So, that monetary policy instrument has limitations. In the future, if a government wants to devalue to alleviate a recession, problems will arise due to the dollarization of bank liabilities. Basically, this is a lesson that applies not only to countries that have a fixed exchange rate or to a monetary union, but also to countries with flexible exchange rates.but in which there are limitations as is clearly ours. - What should be done in the hypothesis of facing a situation like the one described? >> - In this scenario the government should resort to raising taxes a lot or to control spending drastically, and that would generate recessive events, which would make even more difficult the payment of the debt. To get rid of this situation requires a strategy of de-dollarization of the economy; This is a topic that has been talked about for a long time, a lesson we learned in the 2002 crisis. Efforts have been made to reduce this dollarization, particularly public debt, but if you look at bank deposits they are still highly denominated in dollars and part of the credits too. Changing that requires a longer-term monetary program than usual. >> When you have a fairly high inflation for international standards such as Uruguay, that inflation tends to be more volatile. That does not help to de-dollarize because people do not want to be exposed to the risks of the exchange rate. It has been difficult to achieve a more controlled inflation that remains within the established ranges and that does not help to lower dollarization levels.>> The other way to deal with this situation is through a macroprudential policy. The problem is that banks do not internalize that when they are anchored in dollars they make the whole system more unstable. Private motives are not aligned with social benefits, which is why government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation are needed. One can not tell the banks to operate with this or that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you have to give them incentives to do so. That's where the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  happens. - What is your reading about macroprudential management in \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?>> - I understand that Uruguay could resort more to macroprudential regulation. Immediately after the crisis of 2002 it seemed that the lesson was very clear. At that time I worked at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and discussed the need to establish different capital requirements for the case of pesos or dollars, reserve requirements, and insisted a lot on those policies aimed at discouraging loans and deposits from being Dollars. My impression is that the use of these policies has not been sufficiently specified and that we are a little far from applying these policies compared to other countries. For example, in Peru, the reserve requirement for a deposit is 5% if it is in pesos and 35% if it is in dollars. In Uruguay meanwhile, the difference between the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only 6%. The BCU has a great capacity to use its influence to try to soften the financial cycle. Today a very strong consumption boom has been generated that has allowed higher welfare levels, but this also contributes to having more inflation, greater real appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and since salaries are quite rigid downwards, these are factors of vulnerability . - The best tool is a fiscal rule?>> - The fiscal rules help a lot, and the example of Chile is a good case to follow. It is a fact that governments in turn always want to spend more than what is optimal for society, therefore a political agreement is needed to limit future spending. You have to adjust the accounts for the product and for the cycle, that's clear, then, there are different criteria to apply. They are all valid; we must look for the one in which the political agreement is reached. >> - The last monetary decision of the Fed was surrounded by great financial volatility; What will happen to the United States?>> - Clearly there is uncertainty about how quickly the normalization process will take place. It is worrying that, by trying to keep inflation no higher than 2%, an increase in theeconomy. Nor is it known what the neutral level of the interest rate is. In this context, what is done is a risk examination. What would happen if the rate is raised to a certain level or not raised? And what is the true risk that exists. That is something that is done regularly by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . And then comes the decision, which is based on what matters most, sticking to a pattern of inflation or allowing further expansion of the economy. In the Fed there are many who understand that if you let go a little inflation will be very difficult to achieve "bring" back. Therefore, they are very cautious, although inflation expectations are very anchored in society, and that is a very important value. Today in the Fed there seems to be very divided positions regarding the strategy to follow with respect to rates.>> - How much do Trump's public statements affect the decisions of the Fed?>> - In historical terms, it is unusual for the President be publicly expressed about the decisions of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . I am in the Fed and there are no political issues discussed at all. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is completely independent, which is a very strong institutional value that must be preserved.>> (*) Your opinions are personal and do not represent the Federal Reserve of Minneapolis or the Board of Governors of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . >\par \par \uc2 Copyright Grupo de Diarios Am\u233\'00\'E9rica - GDA / El Pais / Uruguay\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190107ef170038v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
