\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Uruguay 'Isn't Prepared' to Sacrifice Growth to Trim Inflation -  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Chief\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2013\par 23:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds background on capital flow restrictions.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Ken Parks \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MONTEVIDEO, Uruguay--Uruguay "isn't prepared" to sacrifice economic growth in order to lower the inflation rate, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Inflation stood at 8.14% in April, outside the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range of 4% to 6%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fought rising prices by setting some of the region's highest interest rates and requiring banks to hold more cash. But it hasn't raised its main policy rate since December, when it increased it to 9.25%.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Mario Bergara said authorities have preferred policies to gradually slow inflation instead of taking more drastic measures that might harm the economy.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody understands that to make an effort to lower inflation about two points could mean an impact on growth, employment and above all social indicators like poverty, indigence and equality," Mr. Bergara said. "Uruguayan society isn't prepared to do that."\par \par \uc2 Uruguay's economy expanded by nearly 4% in 2012, and is expected to expand at about the same rate this year. But that is slower than in the previous few years. Also, Uruguayans still recall the state of the economy following Argentina's financial crisis in 2001, when trade shriveled and Uruguay's banks went bust. Thousands of Uruguayans were forced to seek work in other Latin American countries and in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Today, a tight labor market and high global commodity prices have stoked inflation, Mr. Bergara said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also battling a strengthening Uruguayan peso.\par \par \uc2 "This year the problem of capital inflows has flared up with respect to prior years," Mr. Bergara said. "In the past two years market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were relatively low, and this year they have increased."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds have become popular with foreign investors due to the country's high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The Uruguayan peso has appreciated 1.1% against the dollar this year and nearly 15% since July. It traded Monday at UYU18.97 to the greenback. The peso's rise has sparked concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength will make exports uncompetitive and stifle economic growth.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the government are supporting the exchange rate because if we didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would be much lower than where it is [now]," Mr. Bergara said.\par \par \uc2 Uruguay's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have a target exchange rate. It appears to be an active buyer of dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, with its international reserves rising $611 million this year to $14.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Investors like Uruguay's bonds for their high yield and the steady peso, which has largely avoided the volatility that has struck other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, said Siobhan Morden, head of Latin American strategy at Jefferies.\par \par \uc2 The upward pressure on the peso will likely persist until interest rates in developed countries start to rise, Mr. Bergara said.\par \par \uc2 Latin American countries such as Brazil, Colombia and Chile have tried with varying degrees of success to dissuade speculative investments through capital controls, dollar purchases and other measures.\par \par \uc2 Uruguay began in October to require foreign investors who buy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  notes to deposit 40% of the value of those securities in a special account to make that trade less profitable.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bergara said that measure has been successful, though foreign investors have piled into government bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  notes traded on the secondary market\par \par \uc2 Authorities are constantly monitoring capital flows, said Mr. Bergara, who declined to comment on whether further "capital flow management" policies might be adopted.\par \par \uc2 Frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have left \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a war chest of foreign reserves equal to about 28% of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bergara described the reserves as an insurance policy for when the cycle turns, and investors start pulling their money from the country.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans as early as this year to invest a portion of its reserves in higher-yielding securities, such as those of emerging markets in Asia.\par \par \uc2 "We have so much in reserves that we can create a portfolio where we can relax liquidity and credit rating requirements a little bit...to see if we can get a little bit more profitability," Mr. Bergara said.\par \par \uc2 -Prabha Natarajan contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ken Parks at ken.parks@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 06, 2013 17:12 ET (21:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130506e956004s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw181\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2715\pichgoal600
