\par\par \b \uc2  Uruguay banks solvent enough for crisis- \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2002\par 01:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MONTEVIDEO, Uruguay, July 17 (Reuters) - Uruguay's banking system is solvent enough to weather this year's capital flight triggered by Argentina's worst-ever crisis, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Cesar Rodriguez Batlle said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Banks lost almost 30 percent of deposits from January to May, leading to a 63.9 percent fall in \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  foreign reserves up to mid-July as depositors pulled their savings, fearing knock-on effects of Argentina's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "Uruguay has done a magnificent job because the financial system has maintained itself. The country has respected people's deposits, banks are complying with their obligations and have good solvency," Rodriguez Batlle told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week extended its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Uruguayan unit of Argentina's Banco Galicia , which was suspended at the end of February for liquidity problems, and Compania General de Negocios, a bank controlled by Argentina's Banco General de Negocios. \par \par \uc2  Tiny Uruguay's economy, dependent on agriculture, tourism and banking, has been deeply shaken in recent months by fears of a repeat at home of the economic crisis in Argentina, where a chaotic recession has put banks on the brink of collapse. \par \par \uc2  Despite a $1.5 billion aid increase approved last month by the International Monetary Fund, Uruguay has suffered from a drop in deposits, June's abrupt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flotation and a wave of downgrades from credit rating agencies. \par \par \uc2  Rodriguez Batlle said Uruguay should recover investor confidence if the country, in recession since 1999, reduces its fiscal deficit and resumes economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's fiscal deficit was equal to 4.2 percent of gross domestic product by the end of March, a level which must drop to 2.5 percent this year to receive IMF aid. GDP fell 10.1 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020717dy7h018b8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw181\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2715\pichgoal600
