\par\par \b \uc2  MOODY'S DOWNGRADES BANK FINANCIAL STRENGTH RATING OF BANCO DE MONTEVIDEO. \b0\par\par\uc2 941 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par \uc2 Moody's Investor Service Press Release\par \uc2 MOODPR\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002\par \par \uc2  MOODY'S DOWNGRADES BANK FINANCIAL STRENGTH RATING OF BANCO DE MONTEVIDEO FOLLOWING \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  - PLACES FOREIGN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  DEBT RATINGS OF BANCO COMERCIAL ON REVIEW FOR POSSIBLE DOWNGRADE. \par \par \uc2  Moody's Investors Service \par \par \uc2  Moody's Investors Service lowered the bank financial strength rating (BFSR) of Banco de Montevideo S.A. (Montevideo) to E, from E+, following its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Moody's also placed on review for possible downgrade the Ba3 and B1 long term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  senior and subordinated debt ratings of Banco Comercial S.A. (Comercial). \par \par \uc2  The Uruguayan \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stated that it took control of Montevideo on Friday, June 21, 2002 to facilitate its recapitalization and to ensure that its obligations are met. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would also recapitalize Banco Comercial, which had received an earlier capital infusion of $133 million in February from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its private shareholders after suffering a large deposit loss. \par \par \uc2  Both banks reopened today, Monday, June 24th, and continue to meet their obligations with depositors. \par \par \uc2  Moody's noted that Banco de Montevideo's financial condition has been steadily deteriorating since the beginning of the year. Banco de Montevideo has been hit by a systemwide outflow of deposits as a result of contagion from Argentina's banking crisis. \par \par \uc2  Montevideo's E bank financial strength rating indicates that the bank has very weak intrinsic financial strength and requires external capital support. Banco Comercial's bank financial strength rating was also confirmed at E. The BFSRs of both banks are stable. \par \par \uc2  Both banks' B1 foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit ratings remain on review for possible downgrade, in line with Uruguay's country ceiling for bank deposits. Moody's noted the deposit ratings reflect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong actions in both words and deed in supporting the obligations of both Montevideo and Comercial to date. \par \par \uc2  Established in 1941, Banco de Montevideo is Uruguay's second largest domestically owned private bank, and became the third largest private bank overall after its acquisition of Banco La Caja Obrera in November of 2001. As of December 31, 2000, Montevideo had $691 million in assets, $604 MM in deposits and $52 MM in equity. \par \par \uc2  Banco Comercial is Uruguay's largest private sector bank and the third largest overall with $2.3 billion in assets as of September 30, 2001. \par \par \uc2  The following ratings have been affected: \par \par \uc2  Banco de Montevideo S.A.: \par \par \uc2  Bank Financial Strength Rating lowered to E, with stable outlook \par \par \uc2  Banco Comercial S.A.: \par \par \uc2  Long term Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Debt at Ba3, placed on review for downgrade \par \par \uc2  Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Subordinated Debt at B1, placed on review for downgrade \par \par \uc2  ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED HEREIN IS COPYRIGHTED IN THE NAME OF MOODY'S INVESTORS SERVICE, INC. ("MOODY'S"), AND NONE OF SUCH INFORMATION MAY BE COPIED OR OTHERWISE REPRODUCED, REPACKAGED, FURTHER TRANSMITTED, TRANSFERRED, DISSEMINATED, REDISTRIBUTED OR RESOL