\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3-Uruguay floats  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  as Argentina problems weigh. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anahi Rama \par \uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2002\par 02:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MONTEVIDEO, Uruguay, June 20 (Reuters) - Uruguay floated its peso on Thursday, abruptly ending a decade-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band system as a deep recession at home deteriorated due to spreading economic woes in neighboring Argentina and Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay is seen by many investors as the front line in the wider battle of Latin American nations fighting to avoid contagion from Brazil's deteriorating economy and Argentina, which is churning through its worst financial crisis ever. \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rates of our two neighbors are heaping major uncertainty on us," Economy Minister Alberto Bension said. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed nearly 10 percent lower as surprised investors rushed to shield themselves from a deep slump that has sparked a run on banks and shaken agriculture-dependent Uruguay's long-celebrated status as a stable financial oasis. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's international reserves have plummeted over 40 percent this year amid attempts to keep the peso trading within its former band. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there would be only limited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the new floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund, which has given financial aid to Uruguay in an effort to keep it from spiraling down with Argentina, said Uruguay was a "classic contagion case" and welcomed the nation's "bold" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move, while the U.S. Treasury praised the float as a "strengthened" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Lines were relatively short at exchange houses in the capital Montevideo as many confused people asked each other why the peso's value had suddenly plummeted. Some banks reportedly refused to sell dollars until the market stabilized. \par \par \uc2  Several Uruguayans said their deepest fear was a repeat at home of the problems across the muddy River Plate in major trade partner Argentina, where a botched devaluation in January has put the entire financial system on the brink of collapse. \par \par \uc2  "The problem here is that until Argentina gets its act together, Uruguay can't fix itself either," said Sebastian Batista, a banker, as he waited to buy dollars he needs to pay off a car loan in a country where many people hold debts in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  SHIELDING FROM CONTAGION \par \par \uc2  Shortly after sundown, many people in the capital banged pots and pans from their balconies in a traditional form of peaceful protest against the government measure. \par \par \uc2  Aiming to shield the region from Argentina's collapse, the International Monetary Fund may increase aid to Uruguay by $1.5 billion on top of a $769 million loan approved in March. \par \par \uc2  But any dramatic fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries investors, who fear it could hurt the country's ability to service its approximately $6.6 billion foreign debt. Uruguay has been battered by a loss of export competitiveness due to its neighbors' devaluations and a dive in consumer spending, leading GDP to drop 10 percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Exasperated Uruguayan President Jorge Batlle this month slammed Argentine politicians as a "band of thieves" when asked about Argentina's impact on his economy. He later blamed the outburst on the stress of dealing with his country's crisis. \par \par \uc2  In this country of 3 million people, 15 percent of the work force is unemployed. The economy has never fully recovered from Brazil's 1999 devaluation, and many now worry the free-float at home could spark inflation. \par \par \uc2  The free float decision comes six months after Argentina, Latin America's third-largest economy, devalued its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by cutting it loose from a rigid one-to-one exchange rate with the U.S. dollar amid an economic crisis. Argentina's peso has since tumbled over 70 percent. \par \par \uc2  "They want to obviously try to preserve their international reserves and not spend them on the defense of some arbitrary exchange rate band. So from that perspective, the decision will help them do that," said Marco Santamaria, sovereign strategist at Lehman Brothers in New York. \par \par \uc2  "(But) if the exchange rate were to depreciate substantively, Uruguay's debt burden would become more difficult to manage in peso terms," he added. \par \par \uc2  Troubles in Brazil, fueled by investor worries that turbulence ahead of October's presidential elections will make it difficult to manage the country's hefty $274 billion debt load, have also hit \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Under \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band system, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading within a certain range. Uruguay's previous system of controlled devaluations had left its exports unable to keep pace with those of major trading partner Argentina amid its peso fall. \par \par \uc2  As well as the new IMF loan, Uruguay has recently also won $500 million in aid from the Inter-American Bank, and the World Bank has pledged to lend $400 million over two years. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's sovereign bonds did not react immediately to the news. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices had plummeted as investors shied away from Uruguayan debt while the country has been paralyzed by strikes against tax hikes and austerity measures. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's problems have been compounded by a string of downgrades from ratings agencies, which expressed concern that Argentina's financial mess had sent Uruguay's bank deposits and reserves tumbling. (additional reporting by Brian Winter and Patricia Avila). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020621dy6l0018j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
