\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina says Uruguay approves Galicia bond plan. \b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2002\par 00:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, June 20 (Reuters) - Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday its Uruguayan counterpart had told local Banco de Galicia it would not oppose plan's by its Uruguayan unit to return frozen savings to depositors. \par \par \uc2  Banco Galicia Uruguay, controlled by Argentine financial holding company Grupo Financiero Galicia , proposed earlier this month returning deposits to Uruguayan savers in the form of negotiable bonds or term deposit certificates to help generate liquidity amid a cash crunch. \par \par \uc2  The bonds for savings plan is similar to the one being offered to savers in crisis-wracked Argentina, where billions of dollars were frozen in December amid the country's worst ever economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had earlier called the plan "unsatisfactory" and said the bonds needed to offer better guarantees for deposit holders. The decision reversal was announced after Uruguay freely floated its peso on Thursday citing financial woes in neighboring Argentina and Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Banco Galicia Uruguay's troubles were sparked by Argentina's huge financial crisis. Uruguay has seen plummeting bank deposits and international reserves in recent months in reaction to a deepening four-year recession in its South American trade partner. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's banking, tourism and agriculture-based economy has been hit hard by the recession in Argentina, forcing the government to end its decade-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band system. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspended activity at Banco Galicia Uruguay for 90 days and extended its control over Uruguay's second-largest private bank for another two months in mid-May. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help Banco Galicia \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February after it lost roughly a third of total deposits in December and January, the ripple effects of a run on deposits at the Argentine parent bank, which sent depositors clamoring to withdraw funds from the Uruguayan institution. \par \par \uc2  Last month, three major credit rating agencies downgraded the ratings of many Uruguayan banks with negative outlooks to reflect the government's own downgrade, resulting from dropping \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and the decline of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020620dy6k016af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
