\par\par \b \uc2  Uruguay floats  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  amid Argentine contagion. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anahi Rama \par \uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2002\par 21:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MONTEVIDEO, Uruguay, June 20 (Reuters) - Uruguay floated its peso on Thursday, abruptly ending a decade-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band system as contagion spread from economic chaos in neighboring Argentina and market instability in Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Tiny Uruguay, battered by deepening recession and banking troubles, is seen by many investors as the front line in the wider battle of Latin American nations to avoid spillover from growing troubles in regional powerhouses Brazil and Argentina. \par \par \uc2  "Starting today we are going to switch to a flee-floating exchange rate regime," Economy Minister Alberto Bension said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there would be only limited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rates of our two neighbors are heaping major uncertainty on us," he added. \par \par \uc2  Any dramatic fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  worries investors, who fear it could hurt the country's ability to service its $3 billion foreign debt. Uruguay's peso fell 11.92 percent to 19.5 pesos to the U.S. dollar at the Thursday mid-session. \par \par \uc2  "They want to obviously try to preserve their international reserves and not spend them on the defense of some arbitrary exchange rate band. So from that perspective, the decision will help them do that." said Marco Santamaria, sovereign strategist at Lehman Brothers in New York. \par \par \uc2  "(But) if the exchange rate were to depreciate substantively, Uruguay's debt burden would become more difficult to manage in peso terms," he added. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay, dependent on banking, tourism and agriculture and on Argentina's own recession-hit economy, has been battered by a run on banks and a dive in consumer spending which saw GDP dive 10 percent in the first quarter, forcing a country once dubbed the "Switzerland of South America" to seek IMF aid. \par \par \uc2  Exasperated Uruguayan President Jorge Batlle this month slammed Argentine politicians as a "band of thieves" when asked about Argentina's impact on his economy. He later blamed the outburst on the stress of dealing with his country's crisis. \par \par \uc2  The free float decision comes six months after Argentina devalued its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by cutting it loose from a rigid one-to-one exchange rate with the U.S. dollar amid an economic crisis in Latin America's third-largest economy, sending shock waves across the River Plate to Uruguay. \par \par \uc2  Troubles in Brazil, fueled by investor worries that turbulence ahead of October's presidential elections will make it difficult to manage the country's hefty $274 billion debt load, have also hit Uruguay. \par \par \uc2  URUGUAY FOLLOWS ARGENTINA \par \par \uc2  Argentina's peso has fallen by over 70 percent against the dollar since January, sparking spiraling inflation and putting basic food and goods out of reach of millions of increasingly poor Argentines. \par \par \uc2  Under \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band system, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading within a certain range. Uruguay's previous system of controlled devaluations had left its exports unable to keep pace with major trading partner Argentina amid its steep peso fall. \par \par \uc2  Worried over the impact of Argentina on Uruguay, The International Monetary Fund said its management has recommended increasing aid to Uruguay by $1.5 billion. The IMF generally supports government moves to freely float exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  "It's an important move," one emerging market debt trader said of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float, "The IMF probably asked them to do it as part of the package they gave them." \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's sovereign bonds did not react immediately to the news. \par \par \uc2  The bigger IMF loan comes on the heels of a two-year credit worth $769 million, which the IMF approved in late March. \par \par \uc2  As well as the new IMF loan, Uruguay has recently also won $500 million in aid from the Inter-American Bank and the World Bank has pledged to lend $400 million over two years. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices have plummeted as investors shied away and the country has been paralyzed by strikes against tax hikes and austerity measures. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's problems have been compounded by a string of downgrades from ratings agencies, which expressed concern that Argentina's financial mess had sent Uruguay's bank deposits and reserves tumbling. \par \par \uc2  When ratings agencies lower credit ratings it becomes more expensive to sell bonds, undermining a government's ability to fund its budget. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has been embroiled in a massive economic crisis since the IMF cut off access to a $22 billion loan in December, an action quickly followed by default on part of its $140 billion debt and the disastrous devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020620dy6k010we\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
