\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Uruguay floats peso amid contagion from Argentina. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anahi Rama \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2002\par 16:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MONTEVIDEO, Uruguay, June 20 (Reuters) - Uruguay floated its peso on Thursday, abruptly ending a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band system as contagion deepened from a chaotic economic crisis in neighboring Argentina and market troubles in Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Tiny Uruguay's economy, dependent largely on banking, tourism and agriculture, has been battered by a run on bank deposits and a dive in consumer spending this year due largely to spillover from Argentina's four-year recession. \par \par \uc2  "Starting today we are going to switch to a flee-floating exchange rate regime," Economy Minister Alberto Bension said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there would be only limited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The exchange rates of our two neighbors are heaping major uncertainty on us," he added. \par \par \uc2  The decision comes just six months after Argentina devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by cutting it loose from a rigid one-to-one exchange rate with the U.S. dollar amid an economic crisis in Latin America's third largest economy, sending shock waves to smaller Uruguay. \par \par \uc2  Troubles in Brazil, fueled by investor worries that turbulence ahead of October's presidential elections will make it difficult to manage the country's hefty $274 billion debt load, have also hit Uruguay. \par \par \uc2  The decision by Uruguay - which could see its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly plummet in value when trading resumes on Thursday - comes as worries mount among investors that Argentina's deep crisis is slowing rippling throughout the region. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed on Wednesday at 17.175 pesos to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Under \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band system, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading within a certain range. Uruguay's previous system of controlled devaluations had left its exports unable to keep pace with major trading partner Argentina, where the peso has fallen more than 70 percent since January. \par \par \uc2  Worried over the impact of Argentina on Uruguay, The International Monetary Fund said its management has recommended increasing aid to Uruguay by $1.5 billion. The IMF generally supports government moves to freely float exchange rates. \par \par \uc2  "It's an important move," one emerging market debt trader said. "The IMF probably asked them to do it as part of The package they gave them." \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's sovereign bonds did not react immediately to the news. \par \par \uc2  The bigger loan comes hard on the heels of a two-year credit worth $769 million, which the IMF approved in late March. \par \par \uc2  As well as the new IMF loan, Uruguay has recently also won $500 million in aid from the Inter-American Bank and the World Bank has pledged to loan $400 million over two years. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's economy contracted by 10 percent in the first quarter of 2002. Bond prices have plummeted as investors shied away and the country has been paralyzed by strikes against tax hikes and austerity measures. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's problems have been compounded by a string of downgrades from ratings agencies, who expressed concern that Argentina's financial mess had sent Uruguay's bank deposits and reserves tumbling. \par \par \uc2  When ratings agencies lower credit ratings it becomes more expensive to sell bonds - something that undermines a government's ability to easily fund its budget. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has been embroiled in a massive economic crisis since the IMF cut off access to a $22 billion loan in December - an action quickly followed by default on part of its $140 billion debt pile and a botched devaluation of its peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020620dy6k00s8q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
