\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Argentina's Galicia to inject $43 mln in Uruguay unit. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Patricia Avila \par \uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2002\par 21:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  MONTEVIDEO, Uruguay, June 12 (Reuters) - Argentine financial holding Grupo Financiero Galicia said on Wednesday it would inject $43 million into its Uruguayan unit so it could return deposits, mostly as bonds or tradeable certificates. \par \par \uc2  Banco Galicia Uruguay SA has proposed to Uruguay's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that deposits be returned largely in negotiable bonds or term deposit certificates - a refund plan similar to the one offered to savers by Argentina, where all banks deposits have been frozen since December. \par \par \uc2  It was the latest impact in Uruguay of Argentina's huge financial crisis. Uruguay has seen plummeting bank deposits and international reserves in recent months in reaction to a deepening four-year recession in its South American neighbor. \par \par \uc2  The capitalization plans comes a day after Argentina's largest private bank, Banco Galicia - controlled by the same company - said it would default on a bond interest payment this week and try to restructure some external debt. \par \par \uc2  The $43 million cash injection matches the amount of term deposits held by depositors in Banco Galicia Uruguay, a bank spokesman said in Montevideo. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help Banco Galicia \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2  SA in February after it lost about $500 million in December and January - roughly a third of total deposits - after a run on deposits at the Argentine bank sent depositors clamoring to withdraw funds from the Uruguayan institution. \par \par \uc2  Many of Banco Galicia Uruguay's 13,000 depositors are Argentine and Uruguay is their main offshore banking center. \par \par \uc2  But consumer confidence in what was once considered a regional financial safe haven has been rocked by Galicia's liquidity crunch. \par \par \uc2  Fueling depositors' cash withdrawals from Uruguayan banks is the Uruguayan \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ongoing investigation of Banco Comercial, Uruguay's largest private bank, over alleged illegal capital flight. \par \par \uc2  Last month, three major credit rating agencies downgraded the ratings of many Uruguayan banks with negative outlooks to reflect the government's own downgrade, resulting from dropping \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and the decline of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Uruguayan \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspended activity at Banco Galicia Uruguay, including withdrawals, for 90 days and extended its control over Uruguay's second-largest private bank for another two months in mid-May. \par \par \uc2  Banco Galicia Uruguay has proposed returning seized dollar accounts with an initial reimbursement amounting to 3 percent of all the funds held by depositors, the bank spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  Deposits would then be paid back in nine annual interest-bearing installments either as negotiable bonds or transferable term deposit certificates, he added. \par \par \uc2  The proposal was sent to the Uruguayan \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, two days ahead of a deadline set by the financial system's supervisory agency, the spokesman added. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020612dy6c00zck\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
