\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Uruguay reserves sink by nearly a quarter in Feb. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anahi Rama \par \uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 00:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Adds details; Economy Minister's, analyst's comments; byline) \par \par \uc2  MONTEVIDEO, Uruguay, March 8 (Reuters) - Uruguay's net international reserves fell by nearly a quarter in February to $1.906 billion, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday citing debt payments and a fall in deposits in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  The government also said the economic crisis in neighboring Argentina had contributed to the decline in reserves. \par \par \uc2  Reserves fell $605 million in February, bringing the fall for the first two months of the year to $1.05 billion. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said 50 percent of the drop in reserves was due to a $514 million decline in deposits at private banks and because its foreign debt payments had not been accompanied by new bond issues. \par \par \uc2  Reserves also fell as the government sold $100 million as it tried to prop up its peso in January, days before Argentina devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  To compensate for the loss, the government plans to tap a contingent line of credit for $200 million from the International Monetary Fund that expires in March, said Economy Minister Alberto Bension. \par \par \uc2  "Uruguay will access the $200 million from the IMF in the coming days," Bension said. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay agreed a new economic program with the IMF that can be supported by a new standby loan which the Uruguayan government said would be worth at least $400 million. \par \par \uc2  Bension also said the government would issue debt on international markets to obtain dollars. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's historically stable banking system suffered a steep drop in deposits in February as banks Banco Comercial, the country's largest private bank, and the local unit of Argentina's Banco de Galicia faced problems. \par \par \uc2  Banco Comercial is being investigated by the Uruguayan \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to a judicial probe in Argentina of alleged illegal capital flight following the introduction in December of banking restrictions in Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Concern over the probe led the bank's main shareholders - J.P. Morgan Chase , Credit Suisse First Boston and Dresdner Bank - to announce their support for the bank and a plan to inject $100 million in fresh capital to ensure that it could continue to operate. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Banco Galicia \b \highlight22\uc2 Uruguay\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February due to liquidity problems caused by a steep drop in deposits. \par \par \uc2  The banks' problems shook the entire Uruguayan banking system, which has traditionally received a great deal of money from Argentine account holders who viewed it as a safe refuge for their cash. \par \par \uc2  Bension said the banking system had stabilized since the injection of capital into Banco Comercial. \par \par \uc2  Gabriel Oddone, an analyst at the Center for Economic Studies said the drop in reserves was a serious cause for concern. The drop "is an alert sign for the economy going into the future ... above all because of the problem of confidence it provoked in the banking system." \par \par \uc2  SYMPTOMS OF ARGENTINE CRISIS \par \par \uc2  Tiny Uruguay - whose coveted "investment grade" credit rating was revoked in January by Standard & Poor's as contagion from Argentina showed signs of increasing - sees its economy shrinking 1.5 percent in 2002 under the deal, the economy ministry said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's crisis has sliced revenues in Uruguay's tourist industry and forced the government to accelerate its regular monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations and make sweeping budget cuts. \par \par \uc2  Uruguay's economy has never fully recovered from the 1999 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation of its other giant neighbor and chief trading partner Brazil, which made its regional exports uncompetitive. (Additional reporting by Patricia Avila). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020308dy38016lh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
